INSERT INTO public.sessions
  (id, chapter_id, title, session_number, scripture_reference, content,
   display_order, is_published, published_at)
VALUES
  ('30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000038', '20000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000025', 'When God Wants to Drill a Man', 1, 'Jeremiah 18:1-6', '<p><em>Encountered in a sermon by <strong>Pastor Charles Price</strong>, "Human Frailty &amp; Spiritual Gifts" — Lead Pastor of The People''s Church, Toronto, Canada.</em></p><hr><blockquote><p>When God wants to drill a man,<br>And thrill a man,<br>And skill a man<br>When God wants to mold a man<br>To play the noblest part;</p><p>When He yearns with all His heart<br>To create so great and bold a man<br>That all the world shall be amazed,<br>Watch His methods, watch His ways!</p><p>How He ruthlessly perfects<br>Whom He royally elects!<br>How He hammers him and hurts him,<br>And with mighty blows converts him</p><p>Into trial shapes of clay which<br>Only God understands;<br>While his tortured heart is crying<br>And he lifts beseeching hands!</p><p>How He bends but never breaks<br>When his good He undertakes;<br>How He uses whom He chooses,<br>And with every purpose fuses him;<br>By every act induces him<br>To try His splendor out—<br>God knows what He''s about.</p><p>—<em>Anonymous</em></p></blockquote><hr><h2>Man''s Value</h2><blockquote><p>"We tend to get the wrong impression that our value to God lies on what we do."</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>"What God is interested in is not what we can do or what he enables us to do — <strong>He is very interested in what we are.</strong>"</p></blockquote><hr><h2>Equipping</h2><blockquote><p>"God sees life from the beginning to the end and equips us accordingly."</p></blockquote><hr><h2>Redemption &amp; Being Used by God</h2><blockquote><p>"When man comes and gives his life to God, He doesn''t simply forgive them of their sins and then try and bury all the nasty bits and the dirty bits and embarrassing bits and the bits that we don''t like. <strong>He actually redeems even the bad bits and makes them useful.</strong>"</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>"Embrace circumstances as friends under the redeeming work of Jesus Christ and quit complaining about them."</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>"If God is going to use us, it''s not just giving us things to do — <strong>it''s molding who we are that is sometimes painful.</strong>"</p></blockquote><hr><h2>The Aristocracy of Heaven</h2><p>The Aristocracy of Heaven is going to be made up of <em>interesting people</em>.</p><ul><li>A murderer whose name is <strong>Moses</strong>.</li><li>An adulterer whose name is <strong>David</strong>.</li><li>A liar whose name is <strong>Abraham</strong>.</li><li>A polygamist whose name is <strong>Solomon</strong>.</li><li>A cursing coward whose name is <strong>Peter</strong>.</li><li>A sex maniac whose name is <strong>Samson</strong>.</li><li>An anti-Christian terrorist whose name is <strong>Saul</strong>.</li></ul><p>Their self-inflicted wounds were brought, forgiven and cleansed — they were still part of these men''s history, but became the very means whereby they are able to be a blessing to other people.</p>', 1, true, now()),
  ('30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000041', '20000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000028', 'The Herods and the Governors: A Historical Companion to Acts', 1, 'Acts 12; 25-26', '<p><em>This reflection traces the Herod dynasty and the Roman officials who appear across Acts and the Gospels — their family ties, their interactions with Jesus, John the Baptist, James, Peter, and Paul, and their historical fates. All dates and citations reflect the scholarly consensus drawn from Josephus, Tacitus, and Acts commentators.</em></p><hr><h2>The Herod Dynasty: A Family Tree</h2><p>Herod the Great was the patriarch. He was an Idumean (Edomite) — his people had been forcibly converted to Judaism a generation earlier, which meant observant Jews never fully accepted him as a legitimate Jewish king. His father Antipater II cultivated connections with Rome, and when Julius Caesar came to power, the family''s Roman ties paid off. After the chaos of Caesar''s assassination and the Parthian invasion of 40 BC, the Roman Senate declared Herod <strong>King of the Jews</strong> — with Octavian (the future Augustus) and Mark Antony both endorsing him. He had to conquer his own kingdom by force, taking Jerusalem in 37 BC.</p><blockquote><p>"He was a man of great barbarity towards everybody alike, and a slave to his passion." — Josephus, <em>Antiquities</em> 17.191</p></blockquote><p>His reign (37–4 BC) combined architectural brilliance and pathological brutality. He rebuilt the Jerusalem <strong>Temple</strong> on a magnificent scale beginning around 20/19 BC, making it one of the architectural wonders of the ancient world — the disciples'' awe at its massive stones (Mark 13:1) was entirely justified. He also built Caesarea Maritima, the fortress of Masada, and the Herodium. But the same king executed his beloved Hasmonean wife Mariamne, her mother, her brother, and eventually three of his own sons. Augustus reportedly quipped it was safer to be Herod''s pig (<em>hys</em>) than his son (<em>hyios</em>), playing on the similar Greek words — since at least the pig Herod would not eat.</p><p>The <strong>massacre of the infants of Bethlehem</strong> (Matt 2:16-18) is not recorded in Josephus, but fits his documented character precisely. He died in 4 BC at Jericho of a terrible illness, having ordered the execution of his son Antipater just days before.</p><hr><h2>Herod Antipas (c. 20 BC – after AD 39)</h2><p><strong>Who he was:</strong> Son of Herod the Great by his Samaritan wife Malthace. Augustus modified Herod''s will and refused him the title of king, making him <em>tetrarch</em> ("ruler of a quarter") of Galilee and Perea — the territories where both John the Baptist and Jesus conducted their ministries.</p><p><strong>What he did to John the Baptist:</strong> While visiting Rome, Antipas stayed with his half-brother <strong>Herod Philip I</strong> (a private citizen, not the same as Philip the Tetrarch) and fell in love with Philip''s wife, <strong>Herodias</strong> — who was also Antipas''s own niece. They agreed to marry if Antipas would divorce his first wife, a Nabataean princess. John the Baptist publicly condemned this marriage as unlawful (it violated both Mosaic law on incest and the duty owed to a living brother). Antipas imprisoned John; Herodias''s daughter Salome''s famous dance secured his execution (Mark 6:14-29). Josephus records that Antipas''s army was later destroyed by his former father-in-law, and that many Jews saw this defeat as divine punishment for John''s murder (<em>Antiquities</em> 18.116-119).</p><p><strong>His encounter with Jesus:</strong> Jesus called him "that fox" (Luke 13:32). When Jesus stood before Pilate, Pilate discovered he was Galilean and sent him to Antipas, who was in Jerusalem for Passover (Luke 23:6-12). Antipas had long been curious about Jesus. He questioned him at length — but <strong>Jesus said not a single word</strong> to him. Of all the people recorded in the Gospels, Herod Antipas alone received Jesus''s complete silence. Antipas mocked him, dressed him in elegant clothing, and sent him back. The encounter made Antipas and Pilate friends that day, after having been enemies (Luke 23:12).</p><p><strong>His fate:</strong> His downfall came through his own ambition, prodded by Herodias. When his nephew Agrippa I was made king by the Emperor Caligula (AD 37), Herodias pushed Antipas to seek the same title. But Agrippa had already sent messengers to Caligula accusing Antipas of stockpiling weapons and plotting against Rome. Caligula believed the charges and banished Antipas to Lugdunum (modern Lyon, France) in <strong>AD 39</strong>. Herodias refused the clemency offered to her as Agrippa''s sister and voluntarily accompanied her husband into exile. He died there. The man who imprisoned and beheaded God''s prophet ended his days in foreign exile.</p><hr><h2>Herod Agrippa I (c. 10 BC – AD 44)</h2><p><strong>Who he was:</strong> Grandson of Herod the Great, son of Aristobulus — who was executed by his own father Herod. Sent to Rome as a child, he grew up in the imperial household, becoming close friends with the young Caligula (the future emperor) and with Claudius. His early adult life was marked by ruinous debt, political catastrophe, and near-suicide. But friendship with emperors proved decisive.</p><p>When Caligula became emperor in AD 37, he gave Agrippa the territories of Philip the Tetrarch (who had recently died without heirs). When Antipas was banished in AD 39, Caligula gave him those territories too. When Caligula was assassinated in AD 41 and Claudius became emperor — an accession Agrippa had helped arrange — Claudius gave him the remaining territories of Judea and Samaria. For a brief moment, Agrippa I ruled all the territories his grandfather Herod the Great had ruled.</p><p><strong>What he did to the church:</strong> To consolidate support among the Jewish people, he turned against the Christians:</p><ul><li>He had <strong>James son of Zebedee</strong> executed by beheading (Acts 12:2) — the first of the Twelve apostles to die as a martyr, and the only apostolic execution recorded in the New Testament. James had been among the inner circle of three (with Peter and John) who witnessed the Transfiguration and Jesus''s agony in Gethsemane.</li><li>He arrested <strong>Peter</strong> intending to execute him after Passover (Acts 12:3-5). Peter was miraculously released from prison by an angel the night before his scheduled trial.</li></ul><p>Josephus confirms Agrippa''s reputation for careful observance of Jewish law and his warm relations with the Pharisees (<em>Antiquities</em> 19.292-294).</p><p><strong>His death:</strong> Both Acts and Josephus record it in striking parallel. Acts 12:21-23 says he accepted divine honors from the crowd at Caesarea and was struck by an angel and "eaten by worms." Josephus (<em>Antiquities</em> 19.343-352) records: Agrippa appeared in the theater at Caesarea in a robe made entirely of silver that blazed in the morning sun; flatterers cried out divine honors; he saw an owl — an omen he had been warned about — was seized with violent abdominal pains, and died five days later. He was 54. This double attestation — New Testament and the Jewish historian Josephus, writing independently — is one of the strongest historical confirmations of any event in Acts.</p><hr><h2>Herod Agrippa II (c. AD 27 – c. 100)</h2><p><strong>Who he was:</strong> Son of Agrippa I, great-grandson of Herod the Great — and the last king of the Herod dynasty. He was approximately 17 years old when his father died in AD 44. Emperor Claudius was persuaded that the 17-year-old was too young to govern volatile Judea, so the province reverted to Roman rule under procurators. In AD 48/49 Claudius gave him the small kingdom of Chalcis (in modern Lebanon) and, crucially, <strong>oversight of the Jerusalem Temple and the right to appoint high priests</strong> — significant religious authority without territorial power.</p><p>Around AD 53, Claudius gave him the more extensive former territories of Philip the Tetrarch in the northeast (Gaulanitis, Trachonitis, Batanea, Panias). Nero later added more territory. As Polhill notes, he "ruled mainly over the Gentile population" and never governed the Jewish heartland of Judea, Samaria, and Galilee (<em>Acts</em>, 492). But he was considered the foremost living Gentile authority on Jewish law and scripture — hence Festus''s specific desire to consult him about Paul''s case (Acts 25:26).</p><p><strong>His encounter with Paul:</strong> Paul''s speech before Agrippa II in Acts 26 is one of the longest recorded sermons in the New Testament, and the most explicitly evangelistic defense speech in Acts. Paul directly challenged him: "King Agrippa, do you believe the prophets? I know that you believe" (Acts 26:27). Agrippa''s famous response — "In a short time you would persuade me to be a Christian" (Acts 26:28, ESV) — is grammatically ambiguous in Greek, and may carry a tone of deflection or even irony rather than genuine inquiry. He did not convert. But his verdict was clear: "This man could have been set free if he had not appealed to Caesar" (Acts 26:32).</p><p><strong>His fate:</strong> When the Jewish revolt broke out in AD 66, Agrippa II repeatedly urged the Jewish people not to fight Rome — knowing the war would be catastrophic for his people. When war came anyway, he sided with Rome and accompanied Titus during the siege and destruction of Jerusalem in AD 70. He was rewarded by the Flavian emperors and lived out his days quietly, reportedly dying around <strong>AD 100</strong> — the last ruler of the dynasty his great-great-grandfather had founded. He never married and left no heirs.</p><hr><h2>Bernice (c. AD 28 – c. 79)</h2><p><strong>Who she was:</strong> Daughter of Herod Agrippa I and older sister of Agrippa II and Drusilla. She was first married to Marcus Julius Alexander (who died young), then to her own uncle, Herod of Chalcis. After his death, she returned to live with her brother, Agrippa II. Josephus records (<em>Antiquities</em> 20.145) and the Roman poet Juvenal (<em>Satire</em> 6) confirms that widespread rumors of an incestuous relationship circulated throughout the Roman Empire.</p><p>To quiet these rumors, she married Polemon II, king of Cilicia — who agreed to be circumcised to marry her. She soon left him and returned to her brother.</p><p>During the Jewish revolt of AD 66, she displayed remarkable personal courage: fulfilling a Nazirite vow in Jerusalem, she appeared barefoot and with her head shaved before the Roman procurator Gessius Florus, repeatedly interceding to stop his massacre of Jews (Josephus, <em>Jewish War</em> 2.310-314). Florus barely refrained from having her killed.</p><p>She became the mistress of <strong>Titus</strong> — son of the Emperor Vespasian and future emperor himself — during the Jewish War. When Titus returned to Rome in AD 71 after the destruction of Jerusalem, she followed him. All Rome expected him to marry her. But Roman public opinion was bitterly opposed, seeing in her a new Cleopatra — an eastern queen who might compromise a Roman ruler. Titus, as Suetonius records, "sent her away at once, unwilling though he was, and unwilling though she was" (<em>Titus</em> 7). This tragedy became one of the famous stories of Roman history, immortalized later in Racine''s classical drama <em>Bérénice</em> (1670).</p><p>She was present at Paul''s hearing (Acts 25:23) — the woman who would later keep an emperor''s heart sat listening to the gospel from a Jewish prisoner in chains.</p><hr><h2>Antonius Felix (governor c. AD 52 – 59/60)</h2><p><strong>Who he was:</strong> A freedman — a former slave, freed by Antonia Minor (mother of Emperor Claudius) or possibly by Claudius himself. His appointment as governor of Judea was remarkable and essentially unprecedented; men of his background did not govern provinces. It was made possible entirely by the influence of his brother <strong>Pallas</strong>, who served as the extraordinarily powerful financial secretary under both Claudius and Nero.</p><p>Tacitus wrote of him: <strong>"He exercised the power of a king with the mind of a slave"</strong> (<em>Histories</em> 5.9) — one of antiquity''s most devastating character assessments. Tacitus also records that Felix arranged the assassination of the high priest Jonathan, who had grown annoying by repeatedly urging Felix to govern more justly (<em>Annals</em> 12.54).</p><p><strong>His wife Drusilla:</strong> The youngest daughter of Herod Agrippa I and sister of Bernice. She had first been married to the king of Emesa. Felix, using a Cypriot magician as an intermediary, persuaded her to leave her husband and marry him. She was approximately 14-16 at the time (<em>Antiquities</em> 20.141-143). She is mentioned in Acts 24:24 — she and Felix together sat and heard Paul speak about faith in Christ Jesus. When Paul reasoned about <strong>righteousness, self-control, and the coming judgment</strong>, Felix became frightened and said, "Go away for now; when I get an opportunity I will summon you" (v. 25). The irony cuts deep: a man who had seduced another man''s wife trembled as Paul preached on self-control.</p><p>Felix hoped Paul would pay him a bribe (Acts 24:26) and so kept sending for him — a two-year detention that, under God''s sovereignty, gave Paul relative safety during a volatile period and opportunity to witness repeatedly to governors and officials.</p><p><strong>Was he fired by Nero?</strong> Recalled around AD 59/60 after complaints from Jewish and Samaritan leaders. Josephus says the Jewish leaders would have secured his punishment "had not Nero yielded to the importunate solicitations of Felix''s brother Pallas" (<em>Antiquities</em> 20.182). His brother''s intercession saved him. He was not prosecuted. His son Agrippa (by Drusilla) reportedly died in the eruption of Vesuvius in AD 79.</p><hr><h2>Porcius Festus (governor c. AD 59/60 – AD 62)</h2><p>Festus is historically obscure — almost nothing is known about him outside the New Testament and a few pages of Josephus. By contrast with his predecessor, Josephus describes him as a competent and relatively just governor who energetically attacked the Sicarii (<em>dagger men</em>, Jewish terrorists) ravaging the countryside (<em>Antiquities</em> 20.182-196).</p><p>He could not release Paul after the appeal to Caesar. Under Roman law, once a citizen formally appealed to the imperial tribunal, the case had to go to Rome — the governor''s jurisdiction ended. Stott captures the bind precisely: "He can''t set Paul free, for that would offend the Jews. But he can''t convict and sentence Paul, for that would offend Roman law" (<em>Message of Acts</em>, 367). The specific reason he consulted Agrippa II was not to adjudicate — he had no power to do that — but to help him compose the required written report (<em>litterae dimissoriae</em>) to accompany Paul. Without a coherent charge to write, sending a prisoner to Rome would expose him to ridicule (Acts 25:26-27).</p><p>Festus <strong>died in office around AD 62</strong>. His death created a brief governance vacuum before his successor arrived, during which the high priest Ananus had <strong>James the brother of Jesus</strong> stoned — an event recorded by Josephus (<em>Antiquities</em> 20.197-203) and one of the most historically significant passages in Josephus for Christian history.</p><hr><h2>Nero (Emperor AD 54 – 68)</h2><p>The Caesar to whom Paul appealed. Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus, later Nero Claudius Caesar, became emperor at 16 following the death of Claudius (almost certainly poisoned by his mother Agrippina). The first five years of his reign were so well-regarded that the Emperor Trajan later said no emperor had surpassed them. He was guided by the Stoic philosopher Seneca and the Praetorian prefect Burrus. Polhill notes that "his earlier reign seems to have been more stable" and that "at this point in history, his notoriously bad behavior had not yet appeared" (<em>Acts</em>, 492). This is the Nero Paul appealed to — the earlier Nero, not the monster he became.</p><p>The transformation was gradual and terrible:</p><ul><li>AD 59: Had his own mother Agrippina murdered</li><li>AD 62: His restraining advisors gone, Nero divorced and executed his first wife Octavia, marrying his mistress Poppaea</li><li><strong>AD 64: The Great Fire of Rome.</strong> To deflect suspicion from himself, Nero blamed the Christians. Tacitus records (<em>Annals</em> 15.44): Christians were arrested, tortured into identifying others, and executed in horrific ways — thrown to animals, crucified, burned as human torches in Nero''s gardens. <strong>This is the first pagan historical testimony to Christians being persecuted in Rome.</strong> Tacitus — no friend of Christianity — writes that even Romans who despised Christians "felt pity for them, since they were being destroyed not for the public good but to satisfy one man''s cruelty."</li><li>AD 68: Revolt of provincial governors, Senate declaration of him as a public enemy. Nero fled and committed suicide: "What an artist dies with me" (<em>qualis artifex pereo</em>). The first imperial dynasty — the Julio-Claudians — ended with him.</li></ul><p>Paul arrived in Rome around AD 61/62 and was apparently released after his first hearing. Church tradition (attested by Clement of Rome in the AD 90s, and by Eusebius) holds that he was re-arrested and executed under Nero, likely during or after the AD 64 persecution, at the Ostian Way outside Rome. As a Roman citizen, he was beheaded — not crucified. Peter, tradition holds, was crucified (upside down at his own request) on the Vatican Hill in the same general period.</p><hr><h2>What God Was Doing Through All of This</h2><p>The Herods exercised their cruelty and calculated their alliances, the Roman governors played their politics, and the Emperor Nero pursued his madness — yet God was working his purposes through every one of them.</p><p>Herod Antipas''s mocking of Jesus fulfilled Scripture (Luke 23:11). Agrippa I''s execution of James fulfilled Jesus''s own words that the sons of Zebedee would drink his cup (Mark 10:39). Felix''s greed gave Paul two years of relative safety in Caesarea. Festus''s legal dilemma over the appeal sent Paul exactly where God had already promised: <em>Rome</em> (Acts 23:11). Nero''s court gave Paul a platform to preach before the highest human authority of his age.</p><p>Luke''s account of these defense speeches is not ultimately a story about Roman law or Herodian politics. It is the story of what Jesus promised: <strong>"When they bring you before the synagogues and the rulers and the authorities, do not be anxious about how you should defend yourself or what you should say, for the Holy Spirit will teach you in that very hour what you ought to say"</strong> (Luke 12:11-12).</p><blockquote><p>When you give a defense of the gospel, the Lord Jesus is with you. Rely on him as you make the good news known.</p></blockquote>', 1, true, now())
ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  title               = EXCLUDED.title,
  scripture_reference = EXCLUDED.scripture_reference,
  content             = EXCLUDED.content,
  session_number      = EXCLUDED.session_number,
